 ## Overview
-This method is a lossless compression algorithm for 3D volume data that removes spatial redundancy by predicting voxel values from previously encoded neighbors and encoding the resulting residuals using a truncated Huffman code [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from generic compressors (like gzip or zip) by explicitly modeling the spatial coherence of volumetric data through an optimal linear predictor derived from the data's covariance, rather than relying solely on statistical redundancy removal [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Predictive and Differential Coding](4393-predictive-and-differential-coding.md) by specifying the exact causal linear predictor and truncated Huffman implementation for volumes.
+This method is a lossless compression algorithm for 3D volume data that exploits spatial coherence by predicting voxel values from previously encoded neighbors and encoding the difference (residual) using a truncated Huffman code [S1, unofficial]. It achieves approximately 50% compression ratios on medical volume datasets, outperforming generic compressors like gzip and zip by explicitly removing spatial redundancy rather than just statistical redundancy [S1, unofficial]. Unlike simple delta encoding, it uses an optimal linear predictor derived from the data's covariance to minimize residual variance, and unlike full Huffman coding, it truncates the codebook to manage memory and code length [S1, unofficial].
 
