+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Causality:** The predictor must strictly use only previously processed voxels ($x-1, y, z$), ($x, y-1, z$), and ($x, y, z-1$) to ensure the decoder generates identical predictions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Handling:** At boundaries ($x=0, y=0, z=0$), the corresponding neighbor term is set to 0 (after mean subtraction, this effectively means the neighbor contribution is 0) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Non-stationarity:** The optimal predictor assumes wide-sense stationarity. Real volume data is not stationary, making global statistics suboptimal. The source suggests dividing the volume into an octree for local predictors as a future improvement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory vs. Speed:** The algorithm scans the volume four times (two for statistics, one for difference calculation, one for encoding) to avoid storing the large difference array [S1, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The algorithm scans the volume four times: two for statistics (mean and covariance), one for calculating residuals (not stored), and one for encoding. This results in high computational overhead compared to generic compressors. The calculation of the optimal predictor takes approximately 6% of the total compression time [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On a set of volume data files, the method achieves around 50% lossless compression (i.e., reduces size to ~50% of original) [S1, unofficial]. For the `3dhead.vox` dataset (256x256x109, 13.6 MB), it achieved 48.5% compression, outperforming gzip (36.1%) and zip (33.8%) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Speed:** On an HP 9000 Series 735, compression of `3dhead.vox` took 4.7 minutes, while decompression took 1.4 minutes. This is significantly slower than generic tools like `gzip` (1.4 min compress, 0.2 min decompress) or `pack` (0.2 min compress, 0.3 min decompress) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Removes spatial redundancy effectively, achieving better compression than generic algorithms that only remove statistical redundancy. The optimal predictor automatically compensates for non-isotropic sampling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Assumes wide-sense stationarity (constant mean and covariance), which is often not true for real volume data, making global statistics suboptimal. The four-pass nature and lack of residual storage lead to high CPU usage and poor scalability for very large volumes without external memory management [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Compression Ratio:** Achieves approximately 50% compression (reducing file size to ~50% of original) on medical volume datasets [S1, unofficial]. Specifically, on `3dhead.vox`, it achieved 48.5% compression compared to 36.1% for gzip and 33.8% for zip [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On a HP 9000 Series 735 for `3dhead.vox`, compression took 4.7 minutes and decompression took 1.4 minutes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Entropy:** The method reduces voxel entropy to near the theoretical limit. For example, `3dhead.vox` had an entropy of 8.65 bits/voxel [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Complexity:** The calculation of optimal predictor coefficients takes only about 6% of the total compression time [S1, unofficial]. The dominant cost is the multiple volume scans and Huffman encoding.
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly outperforms generic compressors by removing spatial redundancy. The optimal predictor automatically compensates for non-isotropic voxel sampling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Assumes global stationarity, which may not hold for all volumes. The four-pass approach is I/O intensive.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by James Edwin Fowler and Roni Yagel in January 1995 at the Proceedings of the 1994 Symposium on Volume Visualization [S1, unofficial]. It was designed specifically for biomedical and scientific volume data where lossless compression is required for diagnosis and treatment planning. The algorithm, referred to as `compvox`, combines Differential Pulse-Code Modulation (DPCM) with Huffman coding [S1, unofficial].
